Subject: [PATCH] Introduce production presentation seam: asset descriptors +
 registry normalization
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Step 1 of the Visual Production Implementation Plan (architecture only, no
new art, no visual change).

- Add IllustrationAsset descriptor (vector-component | raster) and the
  <Illustration> renderer as the single dispatch point between a registry
  key and the DOM, so a future authored PNG/WebP asset can replace an
  inline-SVG scaffold entry with a one-line registry change and no call-site
  edits (docs/design/ASSET-PRODUCTION-PIPELINE.md).
- Migrate assetRegistry.tsx to the descriptor model; every entry still wraps
  today's existing components, so runtime output is unchanged.
- Move ChallengeStageShell's inline DesertSkin/CoastSkin/DefaultSkin into
  src/assets/game/stage/StageSkinArt.tsx and resolve them through the
  registry (stage-skin.*), matching every other scene asset. The stage
  background renders in an HTML container (never an ambient <svg>), so this
  call site explicitly passes as="html" with full-bleed/object-cover
  positioning and aria-hidden — otherwise a future raster stage-skin would
  render as an inert SVG <image> outside any <svg>, defeating the seam
  (external audit finding, fixed here with a dedicated regression test).
- Wire PlaceholderOrderTemplate to the same presentation.optionArt seam
  PlacementTemplate already uses, falling back to the current handcrafted
  stone since no content registers optionArt for it yet.
- Add presentation-isolation restrictions (@/visual/**, @/assets/**) to the
  existing evaluation- and domain-specific ESLint no-restricted-imports rule
  blocks, and a new block for persistence + state. ESLint flat config does
  not merge a rule's options across separately-matching config objects for
  the same file — a later shared block covering all four directories would
  have silently REPLACED (not extended) the pre-existing restrictions
  instead of adding to them, and this applies at two levels, both found by
  external audit and fixed here:
    1. domain/evaluation already had their own no-restricted-imports config
       before Step 1 (UI/state/template/component coupling) — the new
       presentation-isolation patterns are added inside those SAME rule
       configs, not a later block matching the same files.
    2. persistence/state had no config of their own before Step 1, so they
       inherited the base config's `paths: ["server-only"]` restriction by
       fall-through. Step 1's new persistence/state block therefore repeats
       that `paths` entry verbatim alongside its own `patterns`, rather than
       only defining `patterns` and silently dropping `server-only`.
  Verified via `eslint --print-config` on one real file per layer
  (domain/schemas.ts, evaluation/orchestrator.ts, persistence/adapter.ts,
  state/selectors.ts) that every original restriction and the new
  presentation restriction are simultaneously effective, and via stdin
  probes that all of them fire on real violations, including nested paths
  (e.g. @/visual/stage/..., @/assets/game/characters/...) — the import-group
  globs use recursive `**`, not single-segment `*`, since the latter would
  have missed exactly those nested imports.
- Add tests for the descriptor renderer, the registry migration, the
  PlaceholderOrderTemplate presentation seam, and the ChallengeStageShell
  stage-skin HTML-embedding fix (both the promoted-raster case and the
  unchanged-vector-component case). 63/63 tests passing, typecheck clean.
- Clarify in comments that the one-line registry promotion path applies to
  static illustration keys only (full-scene backgrounds, ink layers,
  landmarks, stage skins); parameterized renderers that take runtime props
  to select a variant or acting state (character.maitte, character.companion,
  object.slot) need a future, still-unapproved variant/identity asset
  contract and are not promotable by this mechanism.

